  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1148 AM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nModels still doing some bouncing around with the upper air pattern\ninto early next week. A lot of energy will be diving down the West\nCoast this weekend which will dig a deep trough off the CA Coast.\nThis trough will phase with the remnants of Rachel and push this\nmoisture NE into AZ/UT. The 00Z EC solution seems to be a bit of\nan outlier with the handling of this system compared to the\nGFS/NAM and ensemble means. So will lean toward a GFS solution for\nthe short term. Western zones will see some bits of energy and \nmoisture that will bring some shower and thunderstorm chances to \nthe area from time to time. Best moisture remains well west of the\narea but with a stronger storm, some areas could see 0.5-1.0\" in \nthe Gila region through the weekend. Lowland areas generally under\n0.25\". Temperatures through the weekend will remain about 5-10 \ndegrees above normal. \n\nAs we go into Mon/Tue, the system off the West Coast will develop\na closed low and move onshore near LAX and then turn northeast\ntowards LAS. As it does, there will be a moisture tap coming from\nthe remnants of Hurricane Simon. Models have started to come\ntogether and showing the main moisture surge will be east of the\nRio Grande with just Hudspeth Co and north into the Sacramento\nMountains having the best chance for rain. Given the PW's will be\nincreasing to 1.25-1.5\" over the east, could see some locally\nheavy rain. The NBM 95th percentile rainfall amounts out east are\nover 1\" but 75th only around a third of an inch. This shows that\nthere isn't a lot of agreement among ensembles that a heavy rain\nevent is likely but with tropical origins, it is possible with any\nstronger rain band. As the upper low passes well to our north,\ncooler air moves in and highs will return to near normal.",
